  work: [
    {slug:"esagila",name:"Esagila",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/esagila.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Chaldean Empire.","The ████ was the great temple of Marduk in Babylon, the most important sanctuary of the city.","The rulers of the Chaldean Empire, especially Nebuchadnezzar II, lavishly restored it, together with the nearby ziggurat Etemenanki, as part of their rebuilding of Babylon."]},
    {slug:"nabonidus-chronicle",name:"Nabonidus Chronicle",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Chaldean Empire and Median Empire.","The ████ ████ is an ancient Babylonian cuneiform text recording the reign of ████, last king of the Chaldean Empire, and the Persian conquest of Babylon.","It notes repeated years in which the New Year festival was not held because the king was absent."]},
    {slug:"inter-caetera",name:"Inter caetera",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/inter-caetera.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 1493","It appears in the profiles of the Spanish Empire.","████ ████ is a papal bull issued by Pope Alexander VI in 1493, after Columbus's first voyage.","It granted Castile rights over lands discovered to the west of a line in the Atlantic, dividing spheres of exploration with Portugal."]},
    {slug:"wat-xieng-thong",name:"Wat Xieng Thong",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/wat-xieng-thong.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 1559–1560","It appears in the profiles of the Lan Xang.","████ ████ ████ is a Buddhist temple in Luang Prabang, Laos, built in 1559–1560 under King Setthathirath of Lan Xang.","With its sweeping tiered roofs it is the finest example of the Luang Prabang style, and it remained a centre of royal ceremony for the later Kingdom of Luang Phrabang."]},
    {slug:"constitution-of-3-may-1791",name:"Constitution of 3 May 1791",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 1791","It appears in the profiles of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, Grand Duchy of Lithuania and Electorate of Saxony.","The ████ of 3 ████ ████ was a reforming ████ adopted by the Sejm of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.","It was the first modern written ████ in Europe, abolishing the liberum veto and strengthening the monarchy."]},
    {slug:"code-of-ur-nammu",name:"Code of Ur-Nammu",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Neo-Sumerian Empire.","The ████ of Ur-████ is the oldest known surviving law ████, issued in the Third Dynasty of Ur by Ur-████ or his son Shulgi.","Written in Sumerian, it prescribed monetary fines for many offences."]},
    {slug:"hassan-tower",name:"Hassan Tower",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/hassan-tower.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Almohad Caliphate.","The ████ ████ is the unfinished minaret of a great mosque in Rabat, Morocco, begun under the Almohad caliph Yaqub al-Mansur in the late twelfth century.","The mosque was never completed."]},
    {slug:"vakil-bazaar",name:"Vakil Bazaar",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/vakil-bazaar.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Zand Empire.","The ████ ████ is the main covered market of Shiraz, Iran, built under Karim Khan during the Zand period.","Its vaulted brick arcades formed the commercial heart of the Zand capital."]},
    {slug:"al-kitab-al-mukhtasar-fi-hisab-al-jabr-wal-muqabala",name:"Al-Kitāb al-mukhtaṣar fī ḥisāb al-jabr wal-muqābala",aka:["al-Kitāb al-mukhtaṣar"],img:"/images/encyclopedia/al-kitab-al-mukhtasar-fi-hisab-al-jabr-wal-muqabala.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 820 CE","It appears in the profiles of the Abbasid Caliphate.","Al-Kitab al-mukhtasar fi hisab al-████ ████-muqabala, the Compendious Book on Calculation by Completion and Balancing, is a mathematical treatise by al-Khwarizmi written in Baghdad around 820 CE. It set out systematic methods for solving linear and quadratic equations, and the word algebra derives from al-████ in its title.","It is one of the best-known scientific achievements of the Abbasid Caliphate."]},
    {slug:"sheikh-lotfollah-mosque",name:"Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/sheikh-lotfollah-mosque.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Safavid Empire.","The ████ ████ ████ is a ████ on the eastern side of the Naqsh-e Jahan Square in Isfahan, built under Shah Abbas I in the early seventeenth century.","It is renowned for the tilework of its dome and interior."]},
    {slug:"hall-of-mirrors",name:"Hall of Mirrors",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the German Empire.","The ████ of ████ is the grand gallery of the Palace of Versailles near Paris, built under Louis XIV. In January 1871, during the Franco-Prussian War, King Wilhelm I of Prussia was proclaimed German Emperor there.","The choice of a French royal palace for the foundation of the German Empire was a deliberate symbol of victory over France."]},
    {slug:"amarna-letters",name:"Amarna letters",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/amarna-letters.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 14th century BCE","It appears in the profiles of the Akkadian Empire, Egyptian Empire New Kingdom and Mitanni Empire.","The ████ ████ are a set of clay tablets from the 14th century BCE, discovered at ████ in Egypt, recording diplomatic correspondence between Egyptian pharaohs and other rulers.","Written mostly in Akkadian, the international language of the time, they include ████ from the kings of Babylon, Mitanni, the Hittites and Levantine vassals."]},
    {slug:"true-cross",name:"True Cross",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/true-cross.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Ayyubid Empire.","The ████ ████ is the name given to relics venerated by Christians as remains of the ████ on which Jesus was crucified.","A major relic of the ████ was carried into battle by the army of the Kingdom of Jerusalem."]},
    {slug:"corpus-juris-civilis",name:"Corpus Juris Civilis",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 534 CE","It appears in the profiles of the Byzantine Empire and Roman Empire.","The ████ ████ ████ is the body of Roman law compiled on the orders of the Byzantine emperor Justinian I, completed in 534 CE. It comprised the Code, the Digest, the Institutes and the Novels.","Recovered and studied in eleventh-century Italy, it became the foundation of European ████ law and the most influential legal legacy of the Roman Empire and Byzantine Empire."]},
    {slug:"great-mosque-of-seville",name:"Great Mosque of Seville",aka:["Seville's Great Mosque"],img:"/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-seville.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Almohad Caliphate.","The Great ████ of ████ was built by the Almohad Caliphate in the late twelfth century, when ████ was its main Iberian capital.","After the Christian conquest it was replaced by ████ Cathedral, but its minaret survives as the Giralda."]},
    {slug:"limes-tripolitanus",name:"Limes Tripolitanus",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/limes-tripolitanus.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · from 75 CE","It appears in the profiles of the Garamantian Empire.","The ████ ████ was a Roman frontier zone of forts and fortified farms in ████, in present-day Libya, developed from about 75 CE. It protected the coastal cities and controlled movement from the desert interior.","It was a response to the Garamantian Empire and other Saharan peoples whom Roman campaigns never permanently subdued."]},
    {slug:"koutoubia-mosque",name:"Koutoubia Mosque",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/koutoubia-mosque.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Almohad Caliphate and Almoravid Empire.","The ████ ████ is the principal ████ of Marrakesh, built under the Almohad Caliphate in the twelfth century.","Its minaret is one of the finest examples of Almohad architecture and influenced later towers such as the Giralda of Seville and the Hassan Tower in Rabat."]},
    {slug:"schlieffen-plan",name:"Schlieffen Plan",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/schlieffen-plan.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the German Empire.","The ████ ████ was the German military ████, named after the chief of the general staff Alfred von ████, for fighting a two-front war against France and Russia.","It envisaged a rapid sweep through Belgium to defeat France before turning east."]},
    {slug:"walls-of-constantinople",name:"Walls of Constantinople",aka:["Theodosian Walls"],img:"/images/encyclopedia/walls-of-constantinople.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · completed 413 CE","It appears in the profiles of the Byzantine Empire.","The ████ of ████ were the fortifications protecting the Byzantine capital, above all the land ████ known as the ████ ████, completed in 413 CE. Their triple line of moat, outer wall and inner wall made the city almost impregnable for centuries.","The Byzantine Empire profile treats them as central to the empire's survival, until they were breached by Ottoman artillery in 1453."]},
    {slug:"antonine-wall",name:"Antonine Wall",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/antonine-wall.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 142","It appears in the profiles of the Roman Britain.","The ████ ████ was a Roman turf rampart built across the Forth–Clyde isthmus of central Scotland from 142 on the orders of Emperor ████ Pius.","It marked the northern frontier of Roman Britain for about twenty years before the army withdrew to Hadrian's ████."]},
    {slug:"persepolis-fortification-tablets",name:"Persepolis Fortification Tablets",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/persepolis-fortification-tablets.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Elamite Confederation.","The ████ ████ ████ are a large archive of clay administrative ████ from the reign of Darius I, found at ████.","Most are written in Elamite and record the distribution of food and supplies to workers and officials."]},
    {slug:"cyrus-cylinder",name:"Cyrus Cylinder",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Achaemenid Empire and Chaldean Empire.","The ████ ████ is an inscribed clay ████ issued after the Persian conquest of Babylon in 539 BCE. Written in Akkadian cuneiform, it presents ████ the Great as chosen by the god Marduk and describes the restoration of temples and deported peoples.","It is often cited as an early statement of tolerant rule, marking the Achaemenid Empire's absorption of the Chaldean Empire."]},
    {slug:"golden-bull-of-sicily",name:"Golden Bull of Sicily",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-sicily.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 1212","It appears in the profiles of the Margraviate of Moravia and Kingdom of Bohemia.","The ████ ████ of ████ was a charter issued in Basel in 1212 by Frederick II, then King of ████ and claimant to the German throne.","It confirmed the hereditary royal title of Ottokar I of Bohemia and his successors and granted the Bohemian kings privileges within the Holy Roman Empire, including the right to invest bishops."]},
    {slug:"babylonian-chronicles",name:"Babylonian Chronicles",aka:["Babylonian Chronicle"],img:"/images/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Chaldean Empire, Elamite Confederation and Kingdom of Judah.","The ████ ████ are a series of cuneiform tablets recording major events in Mesopotamian history year by year, such as royal accessions, battles and festivals.","They are a key source for the Neo-████ period, including the campaigns of Nabopolassar and Nebuchadnezzar II. They provide much of what is known of the military history of the Chaldean Empire."]},
    {slug:"arg-of-karim-khan",name:"Arg of Karim Khan",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/arg-of-karim-khan.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Zand Empire.","The ████ of ████ ████ is a citadel in Shiraz built in the 1760s under ████ ████ Zand, the founder of the Zand Empire.","It served as his residence and fortified seat of government."]},
    {slug:"jugol",name:"Jugol",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/jugol.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Adal Sultanate.","The ████ is the defensive wall of the city of Harar in eastern Ethiopia.","It was built under Nur ibn Mujahid in the mid-sixteenth century, after the death of Imam Ahmad."]},
    {slug:"harris-papyrus",name:"Harris Papyrus",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/harris-papyrus.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom.","The ████ ████ is a long Egyptian ████ from the reign of Ramesses III's successor, recording the king's donations to temples.","It shows that the temples of Amun owned vast lands and resources."]},
    {slug:"mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba",name:"Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba",aka:["Great Mosque of Córdoba","Mezquita of Córdoba"],img:"/images/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 785","It appears in the profiles of the Emirate of ████ and Caliphate of ████.","The ████ of ████ was built as the Great ████ of the Umayyad capital from 785 under Abd al-Rahman I and enlarged by his successors, notably al-Hakam II in the 960s, with its forest of double-arched columns.","After the Christian conquest of ████ in 1236 it became a cathedral, and a Renaissance nave was built in its centre in the 16th century."]},
    {slug:"bab-al-futuh",name:"Bab al-Futuh",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/bab-al-futuh.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Fatimid Caliphate.","████ al-████ is one of the monumental gates in the northern wall of Fatimid Cairo, built in the late eleventh century under the vizier Badr al-Jamali.","Constructed of stone with rounded towers, it is a fine example of Fatimid military architecture."]},
    {slug:"giralda",name:"Giralda",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/giralda.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Almohad Caliphate.","The ████ is the bell tower of Seville Cathedral, originally built as the minaret of the Great Mosque of Seville under the Almohad Caliphate in the late twelfth century.","It is one of the finest surviving monuments of Almohad architecture."]},
    {slug:"peacock-throne",name:"Peacock Throne",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/peacock-throne.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Afsharid Empire and Mughal Empire.","The ████ ████ was a jewelled ████ made for the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan in the seventeenth century.","Nader Shah carried it off from Delhi in 1739, along with the Koh-i-Noor diamond, after his invasion of India."]},
    {slug:"capitulare-de-villis",name:"Capitulare de villis",aka:["De Villis"],img:"/images/encyclopedia/capitulare-de-villis.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Carolingian Empire.","The ████ de ████ is a Carolingian ████, usually dated to around 800, setting out detailed rules for the management of royal estates.","It specified the duties of stewards, the keeping of accounts and the crops, plants and animals to be raised."]},
    {slug:"behistun-inscription",name:"Behistun Inscription",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Elamite Confederation.","The ████ ████ is a monumental rock relief and text carved on a cliff in western Iran on the orders of the Achaemenid king Darius I, recording his accession and victories.","It is written in three languages: Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian."]},
    {slug:"nueva-planta-decrees",name:"Nueva Planta decrees",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 1707–1716","It appears in the profiles of the Kingdom of Aragon, Crown of Aragon and Crown of Castile.","The ████ ████ ████ were a series of laws issued by Philip V of Spain between 1707 and 1716, during and after the War of the Spanish Succession.","Punishing the territories of the Crown of Aragon that had backed the Habsburg claimant, they abolished the separate laws, parliaments and institutions of Aragon, Valencia, Majorca and Catalonia and imposed the government of Castile, creating a more unified Bourbon Spain."]},
    {slug:"ziggurat-of-ur",name:"Ziggurat of Ur",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Neo-Sumerian Empire.","The ████ of Ur is a great stepped temple tower at the ancient city of Ur in southern Iraq, dedicated to the moon god Nanna.","It was built by Ur-Nammu and completed by his son Shulgi in the twenty-first century BCE. Partially restored in modern times, it is one of the best-preserved ████ and a lasting monument of the Neo-Sumerian Empire."]},
    {slug:"oaths-of-strasbourg",name:"Oaths of Strasbourg",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 842 CE","It appears in the profiles of the Carolingian Empire.","The ████ of ████ were mutual pledges of alliance sworn in 842 CE by Louis the German and Charles the Bald, grandsons of Charlemagne, against their brother Lothair.","They were recorded in early Romance and Germanic vernaculars so that each army could understand them."]},
    {slug:"milan-cathedral",name:"Milan Cathedral",aka:["Duomo di Milano"],img:"/images/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral.jpg",clues:["A work or monument · 1386–1965","It appears in the profiles of the Duchy of ████.","████ ████ is the Gothic ████ of ████, begun in 1386 under Gian Galeazzo Visconti and completed only in the 19th and 20th centuries.","Built largely of pinkish-white Candoglia marble and crowned with a forest of spires, it is among the largest churches in the world."]},
    {slug:"fatawa-i-alamgiri",name:"Fatawa-i-Alamgiri",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/fatawa-i-alamgiri.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Mughal Empire.","The ████-i-████ is a compilation of Islamic law of the Hanafi school, prepared by scholars under the patronage of the Mughal emperor Aurangzeb in the late seventeenth century.","It reflected Aurangzeb's emphasis on Islamic orthodoxy and served as a legal reference in the Mughal Empire."]},
    {slug:"qutb-minar",name:"Qutb Minar",aka:["Qutb Minar complex"],img:"/images/encyclopedia/qutb-minar.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the Delhi Sultanate and Ghurid Sultanate.","The ████ ████ is a tall ████ of red sandstone in Delhi, begun around the end of the twelfth century under ████ ud-Din Aibak and ████ by his successors.","It stands within a ████ that includes one of the earliest mosques in India."]},
    {slug:"napoleonic-code",name:"Napoleonic Code",aka:[],img:"/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code.jpg",clues:["A work or monument","It appears in the profiles of the French Empire and Republic of Haiti (1806-1849).","The ████ ████ is the French civil ████ issued under ████ in 1804, codifying law on persons, property and contracts.","It was exported across continental Europe during the First Empire's period of dominance."]}
  ]
